{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1558638-a-science-fiction-pantheon-s","id":1558638,"title":"A Science Fiction Pantheon\"s\"","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-03-04T10:49:00-08:00","id":1558638,"title":"A Science Fiction Pantheon\"s\"","content":"The basic idea is that Dione, a planet on the far edge of human space was settled a couple of decades before a series of catastrophic events collapsed human civilization. The colony was able to survive but suffered retrograde social and technological transitions.<br /> <br /> Now, a couple of thousand years after the settlement and collapse three pantheons represent the paths the people of the planet Dione pursue. The Solar pantheon, the Lunar Pantheon, and the Stellar Pantheon.<br /> <br /> The Solar Pantheon champions the Virtues of Order, Duty, Courage, and Piety.<br /> <br /> The Lunar Pantheon champions the Virtues of Harmony, Loyalty, Courage, and Vengeance.<br /> <br /> The Stellar Pantheon promotes the Virtues of Intellect, Expression, Valor, Endurance, and Conviction.<br /> <br /> Yes, I'm using the first edition Virtues. They seemed better defined and useful for defining the cultures the Pantheons of the planet Dione promote.<br /> <br /> I'll give a better description of the Planet Dione and the lives of its people later. My main question for now is about the ancestors of the Gods. Scions of which gods go in which pantheons? Should any Primordials be around? Should any Scions of Titans become God's?<br /> <br /> Thank you for your attention.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 03-04-2026, 01:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-05T16:24:05-08:00","id":1558671,"title":"","content":"The planet Dione (named for a goddess of the horizon, or more specifically, the goddess of where the sky and sea meet) is a terraformed world. It smaller than the Earth but has a thicker atmosphere (this isn't odd in planetary formation outside of our solar system). The proportion of land to sea is close to that of the Earth but the arrangement is radically different. Instead of large continents there are vast archipelagos of islands. The largest island is about half again as large as Greenland.<br /> <br /> In spite of Dione's smaller size the arrangement of the landmasses and their topography means that Dione has more arable land and miles of navigatable river than the Earth.<br /> <br /> Because of the low technological base of the population and the long wars caused by the social collapse soon after the foundation of the settlement, Dione has a low population. Only around 300 million people after two thousand years.<br /> <br /> The three main cultures are the Solar Empire which resembles a cross between the Roman Empire around the time of Hadrian and the Persian Empire of the time of Xerxes, the Lunar Tribes, which resemble the tribal peoples of Eurasia as they existed at the time of Alexander the Great, and the Mercantile City States, which tend to resemble Venice of around 1000AD crossed with the Hanseatic League. These comparisons are meant to be social and cultural.<br /> <br /> The Solar Empire and the Mercantile City States have a technological base strongly resembling Western Europe's High Middle Ages crossed with Tang China. The Lunar Tribes are rarely as advanced as the other two cultures but there are exceptions. The skills and technologies of the Lunar Tribes are fitted to their circumstances and are often underestimated.<br /> <br /> More later...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 03-05-2026, 07:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-06T17:08:39-08:00","id":1558691,"title":"","content":"The Gods of the Solar Pantheon are led by a King of the Gods. The gods are members of a hierarchical patriarchal family and their more important servants. <br /> <br /> The Gods of the Lunar Pantheon are members of closely allied clanns (and their is a class hierarchy among the clanns). There is a high chief. As the King of the Solar gods is important as the Son of the father, so the high chief of the Lunar Pantheon is the Son of the Mother. <br /> <br /> The Gods of the Stellar Pantheon are members of a parliament of the gods. There are differences in status among the gods, but the Stellar Pantheon is treated as formal equals. There are definitely firsts among the equals, but the Stellar Pantheon is egalitarian. <br /> <br /> I'm thinking on the PSPs for these Pantheons. Arete from the first edition suits the Stellar Pantheon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-09T05:54:17-07:00","id":1558747,"title":"","content":"Have you looked at the Space Odyssey chapter of Mythic Shards? It has some useful insights about how gods might factor in to alien worlds, noting that therecanbe connections between gods, astrology, and astronomy. <br /> <br /> Of note is your assumption that Dione is Earthlike, not just in terms of its environment but also in terms of its satellite: like Earth, it has one moon. Thus, a Lunar Pantheon built around the idea that <i>the</i> moon is Mother. You could play around with this, making the Lunar Pantheon more pan-theistic by having <i>several</i> moons orbiting Dione. Maybe set them up in a 1:2:4 resonant orbits structure, like Jupiter's moons Io, Europa, and Ganymede. I could see them building a calendar around the lunar conjunctions, using the moons as a sort of three-handed clock: the inner moon would tick off &quot;weeks&quot;; the middle moon would tick off &quot;fortnights&quot; of exactly two weeks each; and the outer moon would tick off &quot;months&quot; of exactly two fortnights or four weeks. Chances are that the moon(s) of Dione won't have the same radial diameter as the sun, making Eclipses either impossible (instead, the moon transits across the sun and fails to block its light) or comparatively boring (the sun is completely hidden behind the moon, with no &quot;ring of fire&quot; effect like we get here on Earth). With multiple moons, lunar alignments could be quite frequent: the 1:2:4 resonance idea would result in an alignment of the inner moon with the middle moon every fortnight, and with the outer moon every month, with the remaining week featuring an alignment of the middle and outer moons on the opposite side of the planet (so, half a &quot;day&quot; away from the inner moon's place in the sky). <br /> <br /> The Solar Pantheon is also technically a misnomer, as Sol is the official name of Earth's sun. But I get why the locals might keep the term &quot;Solar&quot; even if <i>their</i> sun happens to be named, say, Rao. There's also the possibility of Dione's system being a binary: either a close binary, in which case both suns would stay close to each other at all times, and the most common phenomenon would be when/if the suns align with each other; or a distant binary, where one of the suns operates like an especially bright planet. <br /> <br /> Traditionally, stellar phenomena tend to prominently feature the planets, wandering through the night sky against a backdrop of constellations that remain fixed. (The word &quot;planet&quot; derives from &quot;wanderer&quot;.) You'll want to determine what the (other) planets in Dione's system are in order to get a feel for what the Stellar Pantheon might be like.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}